    "turn_text": "I really liked what Benjamin Netanyahu said, and of course, he and his circle know the situation better than people here in America do. He said, To go forward, we have to de-radicalize and de-militarize Gaza. And if that can be done, and the world combines to have supporting a new type of leadership that's not corrupt, and of course not radicalized and doesn't want to arm and militarize, then we can have peace. And maybe that's under an international partnership that looks to enforce this. But he did have a vision for what it would take to have peace. And I think he happens to be right. I mean, if you continue radicalization, if you continue militarization, those things dictate against having peace. So I think he's absolutely critical that those two things are required. And maybe some kind of international oversight to make sure this happens. maybe a carrot and a stick, a carrot being here's some billions of redevelopment dollars because that's going to be needed in the future. We all can see that with our own eyes. But you got to make these reforms before you're given these dollars. And that's where peace-loving countries in the region and people with Western values like the US and the European Union can come in and back that up. Well,",
